    "detailed_description": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>This application allows you to adjust the mouse wheel speed on Linux systems.</strong> </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It will install the dependencies and make the necessary adjustments so the user can use it in a simplified way. The speed adjustments should persist even after restarting the system. </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Attention:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It has been tested and works well on: Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS, Linux Mint 22, Linux Mint 21.3.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Partially works on: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS (not on Firefox), Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (not on Firefox).</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It does not work on: Ubuntu 23.10, Debian 9 and earlier versions.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" > </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Recommendation:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Run the software directly from the installation folder.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >See more information at <strong>Community</strong> -&gt; <strong>Discussions</strong> -&gt; <strong>Run the application directly from the folder</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Developer's note:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >This application uses IMWheel, a tool freely available for Linux. You can install IMWheel on your own and use it at no cost. However, this application will simplify and automate the entire process.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >IMWheel does not work with Wayland-based applications. For example, the pre-installed Firefox on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS uses Wayland, so it won't work, but it may work with other X11-based applications such as Chromium.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >If you encounter any issues with the IMWheel installation, you can remove it via terminal using the following commands:</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >sudo killall imwheel; apt remove imwheel; apt autoremove; apt purge imwheel</p>",
    "about_the_game": "<p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>This application allows you to adjust the mouse wheel speed on Linux systems.</strong> </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It will install the dependencies and make the necessary adjustments so the user can use it in a simplified way. The speed adjustments should persist even after restarting the system. </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Attention:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It has been tested and works well on: Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS, Linux Mint 22, Linux Mint 21.3.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Partially works on: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS (not on Firefox), Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (not on Firefox).</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >It does not work on: Ubuntu 23.10, Debian 9 and earlier versions.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" > </p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Recommendation:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >Run the software directly from the installation folder.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >See more information at <strong>Community</strong> -&gt; <strong>Discussions</strong> -&gt; <strong>Run the application directly from the folder</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" ><strong>Developer's note:</strong></p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >This application uses IMWheel, a tool freely available for Linux. You can install IMWheel on your own and use it at no cost. However, this application will simplify and automate the entire process.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >IMWheel does not work with Wayland-based applications. For example, the pre-installed Firefox on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS uses Wayland, so it won't work, but it may work with other X11-based applications such as Chromium.</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >If you encounter any issues with the IMWheel installation, you can remove it via terminal using the following commands:</p><p class=\"bb_paragraph\" >sudo killall imwheel; apt remove imwheel; apt autoremove; apt purge imwheel</p>",
